From: kusti420 <virtuaalnepannkook@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] usb: gadget: uvc: start frames on an idle isoc endpoint, drop frames with missed payloads

"Use phone as webcam" delivered corrupted MJPEG frames to the host: at 1080p
4% of the frames were cut off (no EOI, a whole number of 3070-byte payloads,
next frame intact) and another 7.5% lacked whole payloads in the middle but
still ended with EOI (ffmpeg "overread"). Neither was flagged as an error, so
the host showed them.

The 5.4 gadget keeps 4 requests (500 us of data at 3 x 1024 bytes per
microframe) queued and refills one per completion, and starts every frame on
whatever isochronous transfer is running. When a completion is handled late,
the controller runs out of requests; the requests appended afterwards are
past their interval and are not sent (missed isoc) until the driver catches
up or the frame ends. With 0011 they complete with -EXDEV; before, they
completed as successful.

Scheduling:
- Queue up to 64 requests (module parameter usb_f_uvc.num_requests, 4..64,
  read at stream start, default 64 = 192 KiB per burst).
- Start a frame only on an idle endpoint (the controller owns no request
  and dwc3 has ended the transfer), and queue all of it in one go with the
  queue irqlock held, so it can't be preempted half way: dwc3 starts the new
  transfer 16 microframes after the first request, long after the last one
  was appended. A 40-60 KB 1080p frame is 15-20 payloads.
- The completion handler refills only to continue a frame that is larger
  than the request pool (with up to 63 requests still queued ahead).
- When the controller owns no request any more, a work item (after dwc3
  ended the transfer) marks the endpoint idle and starts the next frame.

Missed intervals (as upstream 2a3b7af12047 "usb: gadget: uvc: mark incomplete
frames with UVC_STREAM_ERR" and ab9ffa51d5a2 "usb: gadget: uvc: dont drop
frames if zero length packages are late", adapted to dwc3 5.4, where the
payloads queued after a missed one are missed as well):
- On the first request completed with -EXDEV, the frame being encoded is not
  continued (its V4L2 buffer is returned with an error) and nothing more is
  queued on the late transfer.
- Once the controller owns no request any more, the work item closes the
  damaged frame on the host with a header-only payload with the same FID and
  UVC_STREAM_ERR | UVC_STREAM_EOF, so that the host completes it as
  erroneous (V4L2_BUF_FLAG_ERROR, or dropped), and streaming resumes with
  the next FID. If the missed payload was the first of its frame, the next
  frame reuses the FID instead. If the error payload is missed too, this
  repeats with the same FID.

uvc_unbind() cancels the work item before the uvc_device can be freed; it is
not queued once the endpoint is disabled (-ESHUTDOWN completions).

A per-stream summary is logged when streaming stops (frames encoded, frames
dropped, missed requests, error payloads sent). Diagnostics:
usb_f_uvc.drop_missed=0 only counts missed requests and completes the frame
as before.

Used by MonsterROM target/a52sxq/patches/usb_webcam (on top of 0009, 0010
and 0011).
---
diff --git a/drivers/usb/gadget/function/f_uvc.c b/drivers/usb/gadget/function/f_uvc.c
--- a/drivers/usb/gadget/function/f_uvc.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/gadget/function/f_uvc.c
@@ -990,6 +990,9 @@
 		uvcg_warn(f, "%s: video device still open, freeing it on release\n",
 			  __func__);
 
+	/* The endpoints are disabled: no completion queues it any more. */
+	cancel_work_sync(&uvc->video.idle_work);
+
 	usb_ep_free_request(cdev->gadget->ep0, uvc->control_req);
 	kfree(uvc->control_buf);
 
